/-!
# The adic completion of a Noetherian ring is Noetherian

Let `R` be a Noetherian commutative ring and let `I ⊆ R` be an ideal. Then the `I`-adic
completion `R^∧ = lim_n R/Iⁿ` is again a Noetherian ring.

Since `R` is Noetherian, `I` is generated by finitely many elements `g i`. Evaluating
`x i ↦ g i` gives a ring map from the power series ring `R⟦x i⟧` to `R^∧`, and that map is
surjective; as `R⟦x i⟧` is Noetherian, so is `R^∧`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.AdicCompletion.NoetherianRing.solution`: the `I`-adic completion of a Noetherian ring
  is a Noetherian ring.

## Implementation notes

The source gives two proofs, and the one formalized here is the direct one: choose generators
`f₁, …, f_n` of `I` and use that `R⟦x₁, …, x_n⟧ → R^∧`, `x_i ↦ f_i`, is a well defined
surjective ring map, together with the Noetherianity of `R⟦x₁, …, x_n⟧`. The source omits the
details of well-definedness and surjectivity, and those are the two general statements proved
here. A ring map `f : A →+* B` with `J.map f ≤ I` induces a ring map `A^∧_J → B^∧_I` compatible
with the maps from `A` and from `B` (`exists_ringHom_adicCompletion`), because evaluation at
level `n` factors through `A ⧸ Jⁿ → B ⧸ Iⁿ`; and such a map is surjective as soon as `f` is
surjective and `I` is the image of a finitely generated `J` (`surjective_of_comp_algebraMap_eq`).
Specialising to evaluation `R[x_i] → R`, `x_i ↦ g i`, with `J` the ideal of variables gives the
source's map, once `R⟦x_i⟧` is identified with the completion of `R[x_i]` along `J`
(`MvPowerSeries.toAdicCompletionAlgEquiv`).

The surjectivity statement rests on Mathlib's criterion `surjective_of_mk_map_comp_surjective`:
a ring map out of a precomplete ring into a Hausdorff one is surjective as soon as it is
surjective modulo the pushed-forward ideal. Modulo `I R^∧` the completion is exhausted by `R`
itself, the surjectivity half of the isomorphism `R^∧ ⧸ I R^∧ ≅ R ⧸ I`, proved here as
`surjective_mk_map_comp_algebraMap`.

## References

* [StackProject 0316](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0316)
-/

open AdicCompletion MvPolynomial

namespace Stacks.AdicCompletion.NoetherianRing

/-- A ring map `f : A →+* B` carrying an ideal `J` of `A` into an ideal `I` of `B` induces a
ring map on the adic completions, compatible with the maps from `A` and from `B`. -/
@[sga "1 IV 5.8" "partial"]
private theorem exists_ringHom_adicCompletion {A B : Type*} [CommRing A] [CommRing B]
    (f : A →+* B) (J : Ideal A) (I : Ideal B) (hf : J.map f ≤ I) :
    ∃ Φ : AdicCompletion J A →+* AdicCompletion I B,
      Φ.comp (algebraMap A (AdicCompletion J A))
        = (algebraMap B (AdicCompletion I B)).comp f := by
  have hle : ∀ n : ℕ, J ^ n ≤ (I ^ n).comap f := fun n =>
    Ideal.map_le_iff_le_comap.mp (by rw [Ideal.map_pow]; exact Ideal.pow_right_mono hf n)
  refine ⟨AdicCompletion.liftRingHom I
    (fun n => (Ideal.quotientMap _ f (hle n)).comp (evalₐ J n).toRingHom)
    (fun {m n} hmn => by
      rw [← RingHom.comp_assoc, Ideal.Quotient.factorPow_comp_quotientMap f J I hmn (hle n) (hle m),
        RingHom.comp_assoc]
      congr 1
      ext x
      exact factorPow_evalₐ _ hmn x), RingHom.ext fun a => AdicCompletion.ext_evalₐ fun n => ?_⟩
  rw [RingHom.comp_apply, AdicCompletion.evalₐ_liftRingHom]
  simp [AdicCompletion.algebraMap_apply]

/-- Every element of `R^∧` is congruent to an element of `R` modulo `I R^∧`: the surjectivity
half of the isomorphism `R^∧ ⧸ I R^∧ ≅ R ⧸ I`, for `I` finitely generated. -/
private theorem surjective_mk_map_comp_algebraMap {R : Type*} [CommRing R] {I : Ideal R}
    (hI : I.FG) :
    Function.Surjective ((Ideal.Quotient.mk (I.map (algebraMap R (AdicCompletion I R)))).comp
      (algebraMap R (AdicCompletion I R))) := by
  intro z
  obtain ⟨z₀, rfl⟩ := Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective z
  obtain ⟨r, hr⟩ := Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective (I := I) (evalOneₐ I z₀)
  refine ⟨r, ?_⟩
  rw [RingHom.coe_comp, Function.comp_apply, Ideal.Quotient.mk_eq_mk_iff_sub_mem,
    ← ker_evalOneₐ_eq_map I hI, RingHom.mem_ker, map_sub, sub_eq_zero]
  simpa using hr

/-- The completion of a surjection is a surjection: a ring map on the adic completions that is
compatible with a surjective ring map `f : A →+* B` is itself surjective, provided the ideal
downstairs is finitely generated and the one upstairs is its image. -/
private theorem surjective_of_comp_algebraMap_eq {A B : Type*} [CommRing A] [CommRing B]
    {f : A →+* B} (hf : Function.Surjective f) {J : Ideal A} (hJ : J.FG) {I : Ideal B}
    (hI : J.map f = I) {Φ : AdicCompletion J A →+* AdicCompletion I B}
    (hΦ : Φ.comp (algebraMap A (AdicCompletion J A))
      = (algebraMap B (AdicCompletion I B)).comp f) :
    Function.Surjective Φ := by
  subst hI
  set K : Ideal (AdicCompletion J A) := J.map (algebraMap A (AdicCompletion J A)) with hK
  have hmap : K.map Φ = (J.map f).map (algebraMap B (AdicCompletion (J.map f) B)) := by
    rw [hK, Ideal.map_map, hΦ, ← Ideal.map_map]
  have : IsPrecomplete K (AdicCompletion J A) :=
    (AdicCompletion.isAdicComplete_self J hJ).toIsPrecomplete
  have : IsHausdorff (K.map Φ) (AdicCompletion (J.map f) B) := by
    rw [hmap]
    exact (AdicCompletion.isAdicComplete_self _ (hJ.map f)).toIsHausdorff
  refine surjective_of_mk_map_comp_surjective (I := K) Φ ?_
  rw [hmap]
  intro z
  obtain ⟨b, hb⟩ := surjective_mk_map_comp_algebraMap (hJ.map f) z
  obtain ⟨a, rfl⟩ := hf b
  have h : Φ (algebraMap A (AdicCompletion J A) a)
      = algebraMap B (AdicCompletion (J.map f) B) (f a) := congr($hΦ a)
  exact ⟨algebraMap A (AdicCompletion J A) a, by simpa [h] using hb⟩

/-- The source's map `R⟦x_i⟧ → R^∧`, `x_i ↦ g i`, for a finite family `g` generating the ideal
`I` along which the completion is taken; it is a surjective ring map. -/
private theorem exists_surjective_ringHom_mvPowerSeries {R : Type*} [CommRing R] {ι : Type*}
    [Finite ι] (g : ι → R) {I : Ideal R} (hI : Ideal.span (Set.range g) = I) :
    ∃ Φ : MvPowerSeries ι R →+* AdicCompletion I R, Function.Surjective Φ := by
  have hmap : Ideal.map (eval g) (idealOfVars ι R) = I := by
    rw [idealOfVars, Ideal.map_span, ← Set.range_comp]
    simpa [Function.comp_def] using hI
  obtain ⟨Ψ, hΨ⟩ := exists_ringHom_adicCompletion (eval g) (idealOfVars ι R) I hmap.le
  exact ⟨Ψ.comp (MvPowerSeries.toAdicCompletionAlgEquiv ι R).toRingEquiv.toRingHom,
    (surjective_of_comp_algebraMap_eq (fun r => ⟨C r, eval_C _⟩) (idealOfVars_fg ι R) hmap
      hΨ).comp (MvPowerSeries.toAdicCompletionAlgEquiv ι R).surjective⟩

/-- The `I`-adic completion of a Noetherian ring `R` is a Noetherian ring. -/
public theorem solution {R : Type*} [CommRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R]
    (I : Ideal R) : IsNoetherianRing (AdicCompletion I R) := by
  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := Ideal.fg_of_isNoetherianRing I
  obtain ⟨Φ, hΦ⟩ := exists_surjective_ringHom_mvPowerSeries (fun i : (s : Set R) => (i : R))
    (by rw [Subtype.range_coe, hs])
  exact isNoetherianRing_of_surjective _ _ Φ hΦ

end Stacks.AdicCompletion.NoetherianRing

/-!
# The adic completion of a Noetherian ring is Noetherian

Let `R` be a Noetherian ring and let `I ⊆ R` be an ideal. The `I`-adic completion
`R^∧ = lim_n R/Iⁿ` of `R` is again a Noetherian ring.

The source proves this twice. The direct argument chooses generators `f₁, …, f_n` of `I` —
which exist because `R` is Noetherian — and observes that the ring map
`R[[x₁, …, x_n]] → R^∧`, `x_i ↦ f_i`, is well defined and surjective; since a power series
ring in finitely many variables over a Noetherian ring is Noetherian, so is its quotient
`R^∧`. The alternative argument is the general criterion for a complete ring to be Noetherian
applied to `R → R^∧`, whose reduction `R/I → R^∧/IR^∧` is an isomorphism.

## Main results

* `AdicCompletion.isNoetherianRing`: the `I`-adic completion of a Noetherian ring is Noetherian.

## Implementation notes

The source's `R^∧` is Mathlib's `AdicCompletion I R`, the limit `lim_n R/Iⁿ` of the quotients,
carrying the ring structure `AdicCompletion.instCommRing`; no other model of the completion
intervenes, and the statement is a property of that ring alone.

The source's direct proof needs generators of `I`, but no finite-generation hypothesis appears in
the statement: over a Noetherian `R` every ideal is finitely generated. So the hypotheses are the
source's, `IsNoetherianRing R` and nothing more, and in particular `I` is arbitrary.

Stated as an `instance`, the shape Mathlib gives the same conclusion for the two rings appearing in
the source's proof (`MvPowerSeries.isNoetherianRing`, `PowerSeries.instIsNoetherianRing`), so that
instance search obtains Noetherianity of a completion without a rewriting step; the name mutates
`MvPowerSeries.isNoetherianRing`.

Faithfulness to the source, point by point.

* Every hypothesis is used by the conclusion: `IsNoetherianRing R` cannot be dropped, since
  for `I = ⊥` the completion is `R` itself, so the conclusion for the polynomial ring
  `k[x₁, x₂, …]` in infinitely many variables and `I = ⊥` would assert that that
  non-Noetherian ring is Noetherian.
* The scalars and the base are the source's: the completion is taken along an ideal of `R` itself,
  with no localization, no quotient and no change of base ring.
* No canonical map has to be pinned: the conclusion `IsNoetherianRing (AdicCompletion I R)` is
  intrinsic to the ring `R^∧` and mentions no map. The source's `R → R^∧` is
  `algebraMap R (AdicCompletion I R)`, and it is the proof, not the statement, that uses it.
* The instantiation is not degenerate: for `R = ℤ` and `I = (p)` the completion is the ring
  `ℤ_p` of `p`-adic integers, which is Noetherian and is not isomorphic to `ℤ`. The degenerate
  instances this witness avoids are `I = ⊥`, where `R^∧ ≃ R`, and `I = ⊤`, where `R^∧` is the
  zero ring.

